": {"prior_margin": 15.93, "prior_sd": 5.0, "poll_margin": null, "poll_sd": null, "poll_weight": 0.0, "fundamentals": {"margin": 15.83, "lean": 3.0, "environment": 7.38, "incumbency": 3.0, "money": 2.45}, "rating_margin": 16.0, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.69, "baseline_margin": 15.93, "analyst_adjustment": 0.07, "key_factors": ["All ten expert ratings place the race at Solid or Safe D", "Warner's massive financial edge ($16.1M cash vs. $90K) and no visible Mizusawa TV ads", "Prediction markets price Warner at 97-98%", "Virginia's Democratic lean plus incumbency advantage"], "rationale": "Every expert rater calls Virginia Safe or Solid Democratic, markets price Warner near 97-98%, and Mizusawa's fundraising is so thin that he has run no TV ads. With no credible general-election polling, the fundamentals — a +3 Democratic state, a well-funded three-term incumbent, and a favorable environment — point to a comfortable Warner win.", "watch": "A late surge of outside spending against Warner or a strong Republican midterm environment in Virginia could narrow the margin, though not plausibly the outcome.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Mark Warner (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Senator Mark Warner seeks a final term against Republican Bert Mizusawa, a first-time challenger with limited resources. Warner has raised nearly $18 million and holds a $16 million cash advantage, while Mizusawa has raised under $500,000 and has not aired television ads. Virginia has tilted Democratic in recent statewide races, and Warner carries the usual benefits of incumbency and name recognition.\n\nNo credible general-election polls have been released, so the picture rests on expert ratings and markets. All ten ratings call the race Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets give Warner roughly a 97-98% chance. The race draws attention for issues like national security, healthcar